				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kalinin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2203795); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2203795&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606163906" alt="Kalinin" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2203795)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kalinin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2203795); return false;" >Kalinin</a></span> <span class="odate time_1447719114 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">17 Nov 2015 00:11</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2408784">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;extensive&nbsp;remarks</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;hide&nbsp;text</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Good writing means hitting a moving target. For mainlist articles, an essential quality is hitting that clinical, detached feeling that we get by filtering the unreal through the lens of scientific inquiry. That means striving for the clinical tone that everyone is always citing but rarely elucidating. Writing a mainlist article means putting enough scientific detail and accuracy into something to make it believable as a scientific report to the reader.</p>
<p>The other side of that is that scientific literature is boring as shit<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-179238-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-179238-1')">1</a></sup>. When you stuff in too much scientific detail, you oversell the piece, and instead of increasing the reader's immersion, you add uninteresting filler into an article and dilute the good parts, to the point where hunting for narrative becomes not worth the effort. The mainlist article must be just scientific enough to aid the reading experience.</p>
<p>This article is overselling the scientific angle. I get that the exceedingly (by the standards of fiction) complex data and observations are the raison d'etre of the piece. The problem with that is that purely scientific writeups generally make for boring reading, and in this case, I couldn't care less about any of the equations or the specific measurements of the thing or the circuitry or any of that. All of that stuff is so far beyond my experience as a layperson that you could have completely made up large swathes of it, and I (and I suspect everyone else aside from like one or two people) wouldn't be able to tell the difference. That's not really adding immersion.</p>
<p>The rest of the article does nothing to save it, either. &quot;Thing what gets really really loud&quot; is the heart of the article, supposedly, and I'm simply unmoved by that as a premise. As an aside, I'm not sure why we're perpetually seeing really loud things around here, but the fact that I'm fairly tired of seeing those kinds of things isn't helping. The Prometheus Labs angle doesn't do anything to advance the narrative, aside from a pat explanation and an opportunity to have some convenient explanatory literature lying around.</p>
<p>The argument could be made that in-universe, the Foundation would have lots of boring documents like this lying around. And that is factually correct. The Foundation would also have shipping manifests for the month's order of paper towels for Site-19, copies of the services contracts for their landscaping at several covert facilities, requisition forms for safety gear in the Euclid wing, and any number of things that nobody wants to actually read. Verisimilitude is not the same as narrative interest.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Thankfully, the ill-advised supplement was deleted, which gave this an undercurrent of mean-spiritedness in addition to the article's other problems.</p>
