			<div class="content" id="post-content-2408984">
									

<p>Reading through the very detailed Containment Procedures and the opening of the Description, I was prepared to give this the benefit of the doubt, despite a few issues (see bullet points below). I was fine with the formulae, although I have no way to distinguish real from made-up functions, so they didn't add a lot of meaning or interest. But seeing the level of detail set me up to expect a very long article, with a really juicy story to justify the build-up. I liked where the &quot;seal of Solomon&quot; and the SEM picture were going, and was hopeful about a larger story just around the corner.</p>
<p>Then you dropped a two sentence paragraph using the phrases &quot;demon effect&quot;, &quot;demon particle&quot; and &quot;demon gate&quot;, and saying that this was incompletely understood. That, even more than the sum of the smaller issues, was what killed this for me. This article suggests that, in-universe, Foundation personnel need external links to know what IGIS is, a paragraph to explain the &quot;gain&quot; and &quot;tone&quot; knobs of an amplifier, and would benefit from detailed exploration of the maths and electronics involved, but don't require any explanation as to the role of demons in electrical equipment. And as a piece of fiction, you have spent 20-odd paragraphs on background details, only to dismiss the interesting core idea (which involves a huge amount of world-building) without turning it into a story.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Other&nbsp;issues</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;None&nbsp;of&nbsp;these&nbsp;fatal&nbsp;on&nbsp;their&nbsp;own</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<ul>
<li>The basic idea here is the intersection of two very common tropes on the site: an item that performs its function anomalously well, and something that makes very loud sounds. Common tropes are fine, but they carry a weight of history that needs to be overcome to succeed.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>As is common for SCP articles involving electronic equipment, this avoids the &quot;it's Safe, just turn it off&quot; argument by making it operate without power. However the nature of this item means that this one needs to go further - not only does it operate without power, it also operates without connections to speakers or audio sources. Why? If it is designed to operate without power, why have a power connection at all? You say in the PL documentation at the end that connecting it gives &quot;superior performance&quot; but that isn't evident from the Description. And nothing in the article explains why (in-universe) it has an automatic wireless connection with speakers, or why it selects media remotely (or how it selects, what constitutes audio recordings, or what &quot;nearby&quot; means in this context). It doesn't help that this paragraph turns up half-way through the Description, meaning that the question about disconnecting it is left hanging (and is magnified by the second paragraph stating that the item functions as an amplifier &quot;when connected to a set of speakers, a power supply and an audio source&quot;).</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>In addition, the Description notes that the amp has been disassembled and parts replaced. Does this stop the automatic anomalous effects?</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>Even given its automatic operation, the Containment Procedures seem entirely too complex. If storing it in a disassembled state won't work, has a Faraday cage been tried? - that would stop a range of signals from the amp to external speakers/sound sources. Why connect it to a power source at all? If it's going to operate regardless, it seems a waste of electricity to connect it up. And why use a CD player with classical music/easy-listening? That seems like too many mechanical components that could break (as well as music that could vary in volume too much) - why not a radio or an electronic feed? You wouldn't even need a signal - you could amplify static or a single tone. And why are vinyl records needed at all? And if testing is required, why take the risk of moving it to a separate room - couldn't you have tests in the sound-proof locker (which presumably should also be a golden cuboid)? For some reason the idea of a chair in the testing suite really irritated me - it was the final example of why all this detail was unnecessary both in-universe and out.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>The first two paragraphs of the description don't tell us what the anomaly is, and spend a lot of time covering details that could be assimilated by the reader based on the picture, and the sentence &quot;SCP-2167 is a &quot;Gauchito-78&quot; audio amplifier.&quot;</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>The final addendum left more questions than it answered. If this was being used in a private residence, how did the Foundation learn of its existence? More to the point, how did the Foundation learn of its existence in a way that didn't involve a public incident worth mentioning in this report? And what's with the stray &quot;W&quot; left unredacted in the name?</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>The note itself didn't add any new information to the Description - just re-hashed the same information in marketing terms. And the final reference to &quot;infinity and beyond&quot; was confusing. Is it anachronistic (which was my interpretation based on the look of the amp and the reference to '78, although there is no information on when this was made) or did the maker of the amp steal the phrase from Pixar? Neither explanation makes a lot of sense, or seems to lead anywhere narratively.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</div>

