			<div class="content" id="post-content-2411448">
									

<p>Sure, I'll explain how it all works:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><span class="math-inline">$p(\vec x)$</span> defines a scalar field that describes the spatial damping of the effect, defined as the superposition of the damping fields of each potentially affected device.</p>
<span class="equation-number">(1)</span>
<div class="math-equation" id="equation-869410-1">\begin{align} p(\vec x) = c_2 \sum_i \log(q_i + 1) \exp( -c_3\left\| \vec x - \vec v_i \right\| ^2) \end{align}</div>
<p>The most important part of this equation is the last bit:</p>
<span class="equation-number">(2)</span>
<div class="math-equation" id="equation-869410-2">\begin{align} \exp(- \left\| \vec x - \vec v_i \right\| ^2) \end{align}</div>
<p>This section is based on the equation for the PDF of the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multivariate_normal_distribution">multivariate normal distribution</a>:</p>
<span class="equation-number">(3)</span>
<div class="math-equation" id="equation-869410-3">\begin{align} f_{\vec x}(\vec x) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{(2\pi)^{k}|\boldsymbol\Sigma|}} \exp\left(-\frac{1}{2}({\vec x}-{\vec\mu})^\mathrm{T}{\boldsymbol\Sigma}^{-1}({\vec x}-{\vec\mu}) \right), \end{align}</div>
<p>In this case I chose the covariance matrix <span class="math-inline">$\boldsymbol\Sigma$</span> as a constant multiple of the identity matrix (making the field radially symmetric), the mean vector <span class="math-inline">$\vec \mu$</span> to the position of the affected device, and all the constant factors stripped out and replaced with c<sub>2</sub> and c<sub>3</sub> which I chose by simulating various containment arrangements until it worked the way I wanted.</p>
<p>The purpose of all this is to make each affected device have a finite damping effect on the range of SCP-2167's effect. Using simpler laws like <span class="math-inline">$1/d^n$</span> led to problems with speakers located directly behind a cheapo low-quality one not being affected at all due to a sort of 'shadow' cast by nearer ones.</p>
<p>The rest of <span class="math-inline">$p(\vec x)$</span> is a tweak to how the IGIS value affects the devices overall damping.</p>
<p>The next part is actually a little simpler to understand:</p>
<span class="equation-number">(4)</span>
<div class="math-equation" id="equation-869410-4">\begin{align} I(\vec x) = 1 - {\left\|\vec x\right\|}\int_0^1 p(t\,\vec x)\, H\left(-\frac{\partial p(t\,\vec x)}{\partial t}\right)\; dt \end{align}</div>
<p>Originally when I was designing these equations I used a much simpler integral:</p>
<span class="equation-number">(5)</span>
<div class="math-equation" id="equation-869410-5">\begin{align} {\left\|\vec x\right\|}\int_0^1 p(t\,\vec x)\; dt \end{align}</div>
<p>This just calculates the total damping on a straight line path from SCP-2167 to a potentially-affected device.</p>
<p>However, this had a problem, in that SCP-2167's effect could be dampened completely without affecting any devices at all. As a result, I added the second term to the integral:</p>
<span class="equation-number">(6)</span>
<div class="math-equation" id="equation-869410-6">\begin{align} H\left(-\frac{\partial p(t\,\vec x)}{\partial t}\right) \end{align}</div>
<p>The Heaviside step function <span class="math-inline">$H(x)$</span> is defined to be 1 whenever <span class="math-inline">$x&gt;0$</span> and 0 whenever <span class="math-inline">$x&lt;0$</span>. <span class="math-inline">$\frac{\partial p(t\,\vec x)}{\partial t}$</span> is the partial derivative of <span class="math-inline">$p$</span> along the path. For those unfamiliar with partial derivatives, they are just like regular derivatives, but easier - any variable that is not itself a function of t is assumed to have a partial derivative of 0 wrt t.</p>
<p>So basically what this does, is that whenever p is increasing along the path (you are getting closer to a device), ignore all dampening; only count damping when p is decreasing (you are going further away). This also makes it so that a small low-quality device can't shadow a really good device close behind it, since even after passing the low-quality device, p would still be increasing due to the one behind it.</p>
<p>Originally when I posted this, I had had a few typos in the integration formula, but they have since been corrected to reflect what I had intended.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>I wrote up a small program to test the equations, which I intend to upload once I can get it to work on my computer (originally I wrote it as an android app <em>from my phone</em>). Stay posted for an interactive demo of these equations at work.</p>

